---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { current } from '../releases';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';

// The browser screenshot is not a screenshot. `fontina ui` draws text, so the site
// shows the text: the same 80-column frame a snapshot test in the CLI asserts on
// every run, read here at build time. It cannot go stale without a test going red
// first. site.yml lists this path so moving the file rebuilds the site.
// Resolved from the working directory, which is site/ for both `npm run build` and
// the deploy workflow, the same assumption Base.astro makes for `git log`.
const SNAPSHOT = resolve(process.cwd(), '../crates/fontina-cli/src/ui/snapshots/fontina__ui__tests__a_standard_eighty_column_terminal_still_shows_every_pane.snap');
let browser: string;
try {
  // Strip insta's `---` frontmatter, keep the frame.
  browser = readFileSync(SNAPSHOT, 'utf8').split(/^---$/m).slice(2).join('---').replace(/^\n/, '').trimEnd();
} catch {
  throw new Error(`the front page draws the browser from ${SNAPSHOT}, which is missing. If the snapshot moved, update this path and the one in .github/workflows/site.yml.`);
}
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const u = (p: string) => `${base}${p}`;
const repo = 'https://github.com/oddurs/fontina';
// Pulled out of the template literal below: a nested backtick inside `${...}` confuses
// the frontmatter scanner.
const releaseUrl = current?.url ?? `${repo}/releases`;
const releaseName = current ? `fontina ${current.version}` : 'no tagged release yet';
const releaseDate = current?.date ?? 'not yet';
const news = (await getCollection('news')).sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf()).slice(0, 10);
const day = (d: Date) => d.toISOString().slice(0, 10);

// Prose is written as HTML strings: Astro drops the whitespace between text and a
// tag at a line break, which runs words into links.
const intro = `
<p class="lead">fontina is a font manager for the terminal. It finds every font on a machine,
reads what is actually inside it, and makes any of them available to every application
&mdash; per user, in place, on GNU/Linux, macOS and Windows.</p>

<p>What it finds goes into a searchable <a href="${u('/docs/index-and-paths/')}">index</a>:
which faces cover <a href="${u('/docs/getting-started/#looking')}">a script</a>, which are
variable, which file duplicates which, what licence each one carries. It
<a href="${u('/docs/terminal/#previews')}">previews</a> them as real shaped glyphs in the
terminal, <a href="${u('/docs/terminal/#watching')}">watches</a> your folders, and exports
what it knows as <a href="${u('/docs/specimen/')}">CSS and HTML specimens</a> or as JSON
against a <a href="${u('/docs/json-and-schemas/')}">published schema</a>. Parsing goes
through <a href="https://github.com/googlefonts/fontations">fontations</a>.</p>

<p>It makes no network connections, collects nothing, asks for no privileges, and leaves
the system font directories alone. <a href="${u('/license/')}">Free software</a>, GPLv3 or
later.</p>
`;

const status = `
<dl class="status">
<dt><span class="version"><a href="${releaseUrl}">${releaseName}</a></span>
  (<a href="${repo}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">changes</a>)</dt>
<dd>Released ${releaseDate}. Binaries for GNU/Linux, macOS and
  Windows, with checksums, provenance and an SBOM.
  <div class="regress">
  <a href="${u('/download/')}">Download</a>.
  <a href="${repo}/issues?q=is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3Abug">Open bugs</a>.
  </div></dd>
<dt><span class="version">Development:</span>
  <a href="${repo}/commits/main">main</a>
  (<a href="${u('/roadmap/')}">roadmap</a>, <a href="${repo}/commits/main">changes</a>)</dt>
<dd>Status: <a href="${u('/news/2026-09-04-manage-in-the-terminal/')}">2026-09-04</a>,
  milestone M1 delivered: organise, activate, watch, preview, browse. Next is M2,
  typography in the browser.
  <div class="regress">
  <a href="${repo}/actions/workflows/ci.yml?query=branch%3Amain">CI on main</a>.
  <a href="${repo}/issues?q=is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3Abug">Open bugs</a>.
  <a href="${repo}/pulls">Open pull requests</a>.
  </div></dd>
</dl>
`;
---
<Base title="fontina" description="A font manager for the terminal. Finds every font on a machine, reads what is actually in it, and makes any of them available to every application — per user, in place, on GNU/Linux, macOS and Windows." source="site/src/pages/index.astro">

  <header class="hero">
    <h1>fontina</h1>
    <p class="tagline">A font manager for the terminal.</p>
    <p class="lead">
      It finds every font on a machine, reads what is actually inside it, and makes any
      of them available to every application &mdash; per user, in place, on GNU/Linux,
      macOS and Windows.
    </p>
    <p class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={u('/download/')}>Download</a>
      <a class="btn" href={u('/docs/getting-started/')}>Get started</a>
      <a class="btn btn--quiet" href={repo}>Source</a>
    </p>
  </header>

  <p>Real output, on the fonts the test fixtures ship. Only the paths are shortened.</p>
  <pre class="term"><span class="cmd">$ fontina scan ~/Fonts</span>
scanned 5 candidates in 0.00s: 5 parsed (5 faces), 0 unchanged, 0 removed, 0 failed

<span class="cmd">$ fontina list</span>
    id  family               style           wght  wdth  flags  license   path
     1  Amiri                Regular          400   100  ----F  OFL-1.1   ~/Fonts/Amiri-Regular.ttf
     2  Bricolage Grotesque  96pt ExtraBold   800   100  V---F  OFL-1.1   ~/Fonts/BricolageGrotesque[opsz,wdth,wght].ttf
     5  Inter                Regular          400   100  ----F  OFL-1.1   ~/Fonts/inter-latin-400-normal.woff2
     3  Nabla                Regular          400   100  VC--F  OFL-1.1   ~/Fonts/Nabla[EDPT,EHLT].ttf
     4  Source Serif 4       Regular          400   100  ----F  OFL-1.1   ~/Fonts/SourceSerif4-Regular.otf
5 face(s)</pre>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
    <p>Six things, each with the chapter that explains it.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="tile">
        <h3><a href={u('/docs/index-and-paths/')}>Finds and indexes</a></h3>
        <p>Every container format &mdash; TTF, OTF, TTC, WOFF, WOFF2 &mdash; parsed through
        fontations into a searchable SQLite index. <code>scan</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3><a href={u('/docs/getting-started/#looking')}>Answers questions</a></h3>
        <p>Which faces cover a script, which are variable, which file duplicates which,
        what licence each one carries. <code>list</code>, <code>facets</code>, <code>dupes</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3><a href={u('/docs/activation/')}>Activates</a></h3>
        <p>Makes a font visible to every application, per user and in place, with conflict
        detection. It never touches a system directory. <code>activate</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3><a href={u('/docs/terminal/#previews')}>Draws real glyphs</a></h3>
        <p>Shaped, rasterised previews in the terminal over kitty graphics, iTerm2, sixel
        or half-block text. <code>preview</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3><a href={u('/docs/specimen/')}>Exports</a></h3>
        <p>CSS <code>@font-face</code> rules, self-contained HTML specimens, and JSON
        against a published schema. <code>css</code>, <code>specimen</code>, <code>--json</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3><a href={u('/docs/checks/')}>Checks</a></h3>
        <p>Health checks with stable identifiers, so a broken name table or a missing
        hinting program is a finding rather than a surprise. <code>check</code></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 id="browse">And a browser over the same index</h2>
    <p>
      <code>fontina ui</code> is keyboard first: facets on the left, families in the
      middle, the face itself on the right. Not a screenshot &mdash; the browser draws
      text, so this is that text, read from the snapshot a test asserts on every run.
    </p>
    <pre class="term">{browser}</pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
    <p>
      A release archive holds one static binary, the manual, shell completions and man
      pages. There is no installer and nothing to configure.
    </p>
    <pre class="term"><span class="cmd"># GNU/Linux and macOS: put the binary on your PATH</span>
tar xf fontina-vX.Y.Z-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz
install -Dm755 fontina ~/.local/bin/fontina

<span class="cmd"># Debian and Fedora packages are built from the same binary</span>
sudo dpkg -i fontina_X.Y.Z-1_amd64.deb
sudo rpm -i fontina-X.Y.Z-1.x86_64.rpm

<span class="cmd"># or from source, with Rust 1.88 or newer</span>
cargo install --git https://github.com/oddurs/fontina fontina-cli</pre>
    <p class="small">
      <a href={u('/download/')}>Every archive, with checksums and provenance</a>, and how
      to verify one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 id="free">Free software, in these specific ways</h2>
    <p>
      fontina is <a href={u('/license/')}>GPL-3.0-or-later</a> and the manual is GFDL
      1.3-or-later. That is the licence. These are the properties it was built to have,
      each of which you can check.
    </p>
    <dl class="props">
      <dt>It makes no network connections</dt>
      <dd>Not for updates, not for metadata, not for anything. The core and the command
      line contain no networking code at all, and a catalogue feature would have to live
      in a separate package to stay that way.</dd>

      <dt>It collects nothing</dt>
      <dd>No analytics, no crash reporting, no identifiers. There is nothing to opt out
      of because there is nothing there.</dd>

      <dt>It never asks for elevation</dt>
      <dd>Everything is per user. It does not modify system font directories, and a
      font manager that needs administrator rights to show you your own fonts has
      misunderstood the problem.</dd>

      <dt>It reports restrictions rather than enforcing them</dt>
      <dd>A font's <code>OS/2.fsType</code> bits are data <em>about</em> the file, not
      permission the program grants you. fontina tells you what a font claims and then
      does what you asked. Deciding what you may do with a file on your own machine is
      not a font manager's job.</dd>

      <dt>It keeps its answers in open formats</dt>
      <dd>An SQLite index you can open with any tool, JSON against a
      <a href={u('/docs/json-and-schemas/')}>published schema</a>, CSS Fonts Level 4 as
      the style model, SPDX for licences. Nothing here is a format only fontina can
      read.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 id="project">The project</h2>
    <div class="columns">
      <section class="col">
        <h3 id="news"><a href="#news">News</a></h3>
        <dl class="news">
          {news.map((n) => (
            <>
              <dt><a href={u(`/news/${n.id}/`)}>{n.data.title}</a> <span class="date">{day(n.data.date)}</span></dt>
              <dd></dd>
            </>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p class="small"><a href={u('/news/')}>Older news</a>, or the <a href={u('/feed.xml')}>RSS feed</a>.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="col">
        <h3>Releases</h3>
        <Fragment set:html={status} />
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
